**You should not assume the older lines can continue unchanged.** They may remain on the EU market under the MDR transitional provisions, but only if each product line satisfies all transition conditions and the applicable deadline has not passed.

### 1. Confirm each line’s transition eligibility

For each device family, verify and document that:

- it remains compliant with the MDD;
- its MDD certificate has not been withdrawn or suspended;
- there has been **no significant change in design or intended purpose**;
- it presents no unacceptable health or safety risk;
- an MDR-compliant QMS was in place by **26 May 2024**;
- a formal MDR conformity-assessment application was lodged by **26 May 2024**; and
- a written agreement with the MDR notified body was signed by **26 September 2024**, with the required surveillance arrangements in place.

Those historical deadlines generally cannot now be cured simply by submitting a late application. If a line failed any condition, escalate it immediately: the company should not keep placing newly manufactured units on the EU market under the legacy certificate without confirming another lawful route. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/cbb11a6e-f0f3-4e30-af5e-990f9ef68bc1_en?filename=md_mdcg_2021_25_en.pdf&prefLang=hr&utm_source=openai))

### 2. Apply the correct end date

| Class IIb product | Last date for legacy placing on the market |
|---|---:|
| Non-implantable Class IIb | **31 December 2028** |
| Implantable Class IIb | **31 December 2027** |
| Implantable IIb sutures, staples, dental fillings/braces/crowns, screws, wedges, plates, wires, pins, clips and connectors | **31 December 2028** |

Units lawfully placed on the market before the relevant deadline may continue to be distributed or put into service afterward; the former EU “sell-off” deadline was removed. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/cbb11a6e-f0f3-4e30-af5e-990f9ef68bc1_en?filename=md_mdcg_2021_25_en.pdf&prefLang=hr&utm_source=openai))

### 3. Address current obligations—not just MDR certification

- **EUDAMED:** Actor and UDI/device registration became mandatory on **28 May 2026**. Legacy devices first placed before that date, but with additional units supplied afterward, generally must be registered by **28 November 2026**. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/medical-devices-eudamed/overview_en?utm_source=openai))
- **PMS and vigilance:** MDR post-market surveillance, vigilance, economic-operator registration and related requirements already apply to legacy devices. Class IIb PSURs must be maintained and updated at least annually, presently outside EUDAMED for legacy devices and made available to the notified body and authorities. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/cbb11a6e-f0f3-4e30-af5e-990f9ef68bc1_en?filename=md_mdcg_2021_25_en.pdf&trk=public_post_comment-text&utm_source=openai))
- **Change control:** Any planned redesign, intended-purpose change, major software change or similar modification needs a formal significant-change assessment before implementation.
- **Retirement notifications:** If discontinuing or interrupting a line could foreseeably cause serious harm or a risk of serious harm, Article 10a normally requires notification to the relevant authority and direct customers at least **six months in advance**. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1860/oj/eng?utm_source=openai))

### Recommended portfolio action

Create a line-by-line matrix now covering:

1. transition eligibility evidence;
2. implantable/non-implantable deadline;
3. MDR application and notified-body status;
4. EUDAMED registration status;
5. significant-change constraints;
6. annual PSUR/PMS status; and
7. decision to **MDR-certify, freeze and run out, or discontinue**.

For 2028 lines, plan MDR certification well before **31 December 2028** rather than treating that date as the target certificate date. Any line without clear evidence of meeting the 2024 transition conditions should be treated as an immediate compliance exception.

Yes. For **calendar 2027**, I would plan around three firm reporting streams and two conditional ones.

| Priority | Reporting requirement | What the systems roadmap should include |
|---|---|---|
| **1 — urgent** | **DAC8/CARF: first return due 31 May 2027 for 2026 activity** | A customer tax-residency/TIN master; controlling-person data; reportability and exclusion rules; transaction aggregation by customer and crypto-asset; fiat/EUR valuation; corrections; XML/output capability; and generation of the copy that must also be sent to each reported customer by 31 May. Ireland requires RCASPs to register by **31 December 2026**, and pre-existing users generally need to be documented by **1 January 2027**. This requires remediation during 2026, not just a 2027 project. ([revenue.ie](https://www.revenue.ie/en/companies-and-charities/international-tax/aeoi/dac8/index.aspx)) |
| **2 — high** | **Central Bank MiCA CASP Return** | Automate the quarterly CSR workbook from the ledger, trading platform, CRM and custody systems. It covers transaction/client metrics, income, off-balance-sheet exposures, own funds, fixed overheads and detailed client-asset information, including custodians, outsourcing, jurisdictions and custody wallets. Returns are due **42 calendar days after each quarter-end** and require executive/two-person sign-off. ([centralbank.ie](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/micar/guidance-notes-for-crypto-assets-service-providers-return.pdf?sfvrsn=20e7731a_1)) |
| **3 — high** | **AML/CFT Risk Evaluation Questionnaire** | Build a reusable regulatory-data layer rather than another manual spreadsheet. The CASP REQ is XML-only and captures detailed customer, geography, product, channel and transaction-risk metrics. The Central Bank says future reference dates and deadlines will be notified; the design is intended to align with future AMLA data requests. ([edit.centralbank.ie](https://edit.centralbank.ie/regulation/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-the-financing-of-terrorism/sector-specific-ml-tf-risk-evaluation-questionnaire)) |
| **4 — medium** | **DORA Register of Information** | Maintain a live contract-level inventory of ICT providers, subcontracting chains, services, critical functions and provider identifiers. The annual submission uses **XBRL OIM-CSV**, with strict validations, so this should feed from procurement/vendor-management systems rather than be reconstructed annually. ([edit.centralbank.ie](https://edit.centralbank.ie/regulation/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora/reporting-registers-of-information)) |
| **5 — horizon** | **Irish e-invoicing and real-time VAT reporting** | If Revenue treats the company as a “large corporate,” design AP/AR changes during 2027: structured invoices compliant with **EN16931**, supplier/customer readiness and transaction-data reporting. Mandatory Phase One starts **1 November 2028**, while all Irish businesses must then be able to receive structured e-invoices. ([revenue.ie](https://www.revenue.ie/en/corporate/press-office/press-releases/2026/pr-021026-phase-one-vat-modernisation.aspx?utm_source=openai)) |

### Recommended architecture

Build one governed **regulatory reporting data mart** with:

- immutable transaction-level records linked to customer, wallet, asset and legal entity;
- consistent definitions for customer status, residency, active client, transaction type and service type;
- historical EUR valuations with source and timestamp;
- reconciliation to the general ledger, custody records and blockchain data;
- effective-dated customer tax and controlling-person information;
- maker/checker approval, submission evidence and correction history;
- configurable outputs for Excel, XML and XBRL OIM-CSV.

The immediate critical path is **DAC8/CARF**: confirm that all reportable 2026 transactions can be reconstructed and that missing TIN, residence and controlling-person information can be remediated before **1 January 2027**.

Also assess separately whether the group is within **Pillar Two**—generally relevant to groups at or above the €750 million threshold—or CSRD. If so, GIR/TIR and sustainability-reporting data would need their own workstreams. ([revenue.ie](https://www.revenue.ie/en/companies-and-charities/pillar-two/top-up/compliance.aspx?utm_source=openai))

Yes. The main 2027 reporting build should be **DAC8/CARF**, with MiCA and DORA data capabilities alongside it.

### 1. DAC8 crypto tax reporting — highest priority

DAC8 requires crypto-asset service providers to report EU-resident clients’ crypto transactions annually. The first reporting cycle covers **calendar year 2026**, with reporting/information exchange beginning in **2027**: [New Directive on tax transparency to help Member States shine a light on the crypto asset sector](https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/news/new-directive-tax-transparency-help-member-states-shine-light-crypto-asset-sector-2023-10-17_en) and [DAC8 implementation guidance](https://www.bmf.gv.at/rechtsnews/steuern-rechtsnews/aktuelle-infos-und-erlaesse/Fachinformationen---Internationales-Steuerrecht/umsetzungsfragen-dac8-implementierung.html).

Given your EU-resident client base, build:

- tax-residency, TIN and customer/entity classification data;
- transaction-level capture covering exchanges, acquisitions, disposals and transfers;
- consistent fiat valuation, asset identifiers and timestamping;
- annual aggregation by client, asset and transaction type;
- data-quality rules, corrections, audit trail and report reconciliation;
- customer-notification capability;
- schema-based report generation rather than spreadsheets.

**Important:** this is already a 2026 data-capture requirement, not something to start in 2027. If the required fields have not been retained since 1 January 2026, prioritise a gap assessment and backfill now. I could not find a definitive Irish Revenue filing schema or Irish filing deadline in the corpus, so keep the output layer configurable pending Revenue specifications.

### 2. MiCA finance and safeguarding data

MiCA imposes prudential, governance, safeguarding and data-transparency obligations on CASPs: [Central Bank of Ireland MiCAR industry briefing](https://www.centralbank.ie/news/article/speech-industry-briefing-on-micar-remarks-by-deputy-governor-derville-rowland-18-jul-2024).

Create a regulator-ready finance mart supporting:

- own-funds and fixed-overhead calculations;
- client-money and client-crypto reconciliations;
- segregation and safeguarding evidence;
- revenue and transaction volumes by service and jurisdiction;
- complaints, outsourcing and operational-risk metrics;
- reproducible period-end snapshots and sign-off workflows.

The Central Bank uses its Portal for regulatory returns, so access, permissions and submission ownership should also be formalised: [Central Bank of Ireland Portal](https://www.centralbank.ie/regulation/central-bank-portal). I did not find a published Ireland-specific CASP return pack or filing cadence, so avoid hard-coding templates until the CBI issues them.

### 3. DORA reporting support

DORA requires major ICT incident reporting and a structured register of ICT third-party contractual arrangements: [CBI DORA FAQs](https://www.centralbank.ie/regulation/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora/dora---frequently-asked-questions).

Build or integrate:

- a contract-level ICT supplier register with entity, service, location and subcontractor relationships;
- controlled exports in the prescribed structured format;
- incident data capture aligned to regulatory notifications;
- ownership and reconciliation between procurement, finance, legal and operational-risk systems.

### Recommended sequencing

1. **Immediately:** DAC8 data completeness/backfill and Irish filing-specification monitoring.  
2. **Q3–Q4 2026:** DAC8 reporting engine, reconciliation and dry run.  
3. **For 2027:** automate MiCA prudential/safeguarding packs and DORA structured exports.  
4. Use a shared regulatory data layer so customer, transaction, valuation and legal-entity data are not separately rebuilt for each regime.

Yes. **DAC8/CARF should be the main 2027 reporting build**, because the report will cover transactions occurring **now, during 2026**.

### 1. DAC8 crypto-tax reporting — priority 0

DAC8 requires EU crypto-asset providers to report EU-resident clients’ transactions annually and notify clients that their information will be shared with tax authorities: [New Directive on tax transparency](https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/news/new-directive-tax-transparency-help-member-states-shine-light-crypto-asset-sector-2023-10-17_en). The first exchange is expected in **2027 for calendar-year 2026 data**: [DAC8 implementation guidance](https://www.bmf.gv.at/rechtsnews/steuern-rechtsnews/aktuelle-infos-und-erlaesse/Fachinformationen---Internationales-Steuerrecht/umsetzungsfragen-dac8-implementierung.html).

I would roadmap:

- customer tax-residence, TIN and entity/controlling-person data;
- transaction classification across exchange and transfer services;
- consistent asset identifiers, quantities and fiat valuation;
- annual aggregation by customer, jurisdiction, asset and transaction type;
- report generation, validation, corrections and resubmissions;
- customer-notification workflow;
- immutable audit trail linking reported figures to ledger transactions and KYC evidence.

This should be implemented against **2026 historical data immediately**, rather than waiting for the 2027 filing window.

The corpus did not surface final Irish Revenue filing specifications or the precise Irish submission deadline. Keep the output layer configurable until Revenue confirms its schema, portal and validation rules.

### 2. Reuse the data layer for existing regulatory reporting

- **DORA:** maintain structured data on ICT third-party contracts and support major-incident reporting. The Central Bank confirms both requirements: [DORA FAQs](https://www.centralbank.ie/regulation/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora/dora---frequently-asked-questions). Integrating procurement/vendor-master data would reduce manual register preparation.
- **MiCA:** ensure finance can evidence ongoing prudential resources, client-asset segregation and regulatory information requested during supervision. These are continuing obligations rather than a new 2027 regime: [Central Bank MiCAR FAQs](https://www.centralbank.ie/regulation/markets-in-crypto-assets-regulation/micar---frequently-asked-questions).
- **ART/EMT data:** if you support stablecoins, retain holder and transaction-level data in a form that can support MiCA issuer reporting requests; the supervisory templates cover holder, transaction, liquidity and related data collected from CASPs: [Circular CSSF 25/885](https://www.cssf.lu/en/Document/circular-cssf-25-885/).

**Recommended sequencing:** DAC8 data-gap remediation now → parallel 2026 backfill/reconciliation → reporting engine and controls in early 2027 → Irish Revenue submission adapter once specifications are final.

Yes. For this cycle, I would strengthen the methodology in the following areas—particularly for CASP clients.

### Priority additions for 2026

1. **MiCA authorisation as a gating factor**
   - The EU transitional period ended on **1 July 2026**. A CASP serving EU clients should now appear as authorised in ESMA’s MiCA register; the register also identifies reported non-compliant entities.
   - Treat absent, refused, withdrawn or out-of-scope authorisation as an escalation or exit trigger, rather than merely another weighted factor.
   - Compare actual products, jurisdictions and websites with the authorised scope. ([esma.europa.eu](https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2026-04/ESMA75-113276571-1679_Statement_on_the_end_of_transitional_periods_under_MiCA.pdf?utm_source=openai))

2. **A distinct restrictive-measures exposure assessment**
   - The EBA’s sanctions-control Guidelines have applied since **30 December 2025**. Consider separate exposure factors for customer/owner links to sanctioned or circumvention jurisdictions, affected industries, products, channels and transaction patterns.
   - Keep this separate from the ML/TF score: a low AML rating cannot dilute the obligation to implement sanctions. ([eba.europa.eu](https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/single-rulebook/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism/guidelines-internal-policies-procedures-and-controls-ensure-implementation-union-and-national?version=2024&utm_source=openai))

3. **More granular crypto business-model risk**
   Avoid a single “CASP uplift.” Differentiate:
   - stablecoins, particularly cross-chain and P2P use;
   - self-hosted-wallet exposure;
   - bridges, mixers, privacy-enhancing assets and chain-hopping;
   - DeFi interfaces, crypto lending and staking;
   - nested or omnibus services and “payable-through” access;
   - token issuance/redemption and the ability to freeze or burn assets;
   - exposure to ransomware, scams and state-linked cybercrime.

   FATF’s March 2026 report particularly highlights stablecoins, unhosted wallets and cross-chain activity as developing vulnerabilities. These should be contextual factors rather than automatic high-risk classifications. ([fatf-gafi.org](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Virtualassets/targeted-report-stablecoins-unhosted-wallets.html?utm_source=openai))

4. **Evidence-based assessment of Travel Rule controls**
   Include measurable indicators such as:
   - percentage of transfers with missing/incomplete data;
   - rejection, suspension and return rates;
   - self-hosted-address verification methods;
   - counterparty-CASP due diligence;
   - blockchain-analytics coverage across assets and chains;
   - treatment of unlicensed counterparties and repeated failures.

   The EU Travel Rule and EBA Guidelines have applied to crypto transfers since **30 December 2024**. ([eba.europa.eu](https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/single-rulebook/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism/guidelines-information-requirements-relation-transfers-funds-and-certain-crypto-assets-transfers?version=2024&utm_source=openai))

5. **Proliferation-financing and sanctions-evasion risk**
   Add PF explicitly rather than subsuming it under geography. Relevant indicators include dual-use sectors, procurement networks, trans-shipment hubs, opaque trade flows, state-linked cyber activity and crypto conversion involving sanctioned actors. FATF standards expressly extend PF risk assessment to virtual-asset activities and service providers. ([fatf-gafi.org](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Financingofproliferation/Proliferation-financing-risk-assessment-mitigation.html?utm_source=openai))

6. **Dynamic geographic risk**
   Use automated feeds rather than a static annual table. The EU list changed materially in 2025–26: for example, Russia, Bolivia and the British Virgin Islands entered the list on **29 January 2026**, while several jurisdictions were removed.

   Removal from the EU or FATF list should remove the mandatory-listing treatment, but should not automatically produce a low-risk score; retain independent measures for corruption, conflict, sanctions circumvention, regulatory effectiveness and the client’s actual transaction corridors. ([finance.ec.europa.eu](https://finance.ec.europa.eu/financial-crime/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism-international-level_en?utm_source=openai))

7. **Control effectiveness and data confidence as separate dimensions**
   Calculate:
   - **inherent risk**;
   - **demonstrated control effectiveness**; and
   - **data/evidence confidence**.

   Controls should only reduce risk where supported by testing—for example alert outcomes, Travel Rule failure rates, wallet-screening coverage, regulatory findings and sample-based file reviews. Missing or unverifiable information should cap the residual rating rather than receive a neutral score.

8. **Prepare for the AMLR/AMLA framework**
   The AML Regulation applies from **10 July 2027** and expressly brings targeted-financial-sanctions evasion into business-wide risk assessment. AMLA’s draft CDD standards were consulted on in early 2026 but are not yet final, so they are best used for data-field and process-gap analysis, not treated as binding requirements. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32024R1624))

### Methodology changes I would implement

- Create a dedicated CASP segment with sub-models by service type.
- Introduce **non-compensatory overrides** for:
  - unauthorised EU activity;
  - activity outside licence scope;
  - persistent Travel Rule failure;
  - undisclosed nested/omnibus relationships;
  - inability to establish ownership/control;
  - inadequate wallet or sanctions-screening capability.
- Add event-driven reviews for licence changes, new products/chains, ownership changes, regulator action, material volume/corridor shifts and adverse alert trends.
- Include portfolio-level concentration and interconnectedness—not just individual-client risk.
- Back-test ratings against SARs, exits, regulator findings, sanctions alerts and material transaction-monitoring cases.

The most important practical change is to move from a largely **customer-and-geography score** to a model that also captures **authorisation status, actual crypto activity, sanctions/PF exposure, control performance and evidence quality**.

Yes. The main additions for this cycle should be **crypto-specific risk differentiation, sanctions exposure, and control-effectiveness indicators**, rather than simply increasing the score for an entire sector.

### Priority methodology changes

1. **Create a dedicated CASP/crypto-client module**

   The EBA’s amended risk-factor guidance now expressly covers crypto-assets and customers providing crypto services—including providers not authorised under MiCA. The module should distinguish:
   - MiCA authorisation or valid transitional/legal status in each relevant Member State;
   - services offered: exchange, custody, transfers, brokerage, issuance, staking/lending and DeFi interfaces;
   - exposure to self-hosted wallets, mixers/tumblers, privacy-enhancing assets and cross-chain services;
   - customer and transaction exposure to high-risk or weakly regulated jurisdictions;
   - quality of blockchain analytics, transaction monitoring and wallet-screening arrangements;
   - reliance on third-party CASPs or white-label infrastructure.

   Source: [EBA Final Report—Guidelines Amending the ML/TF Risk Factors Guidelines](https://www.eba.europa.eu/legacy/regulation-and-policy/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-1).

2. **Score Travel Rule capability and performance**

   For CASP clients, do not assess only whether a policy exists. Include operational indicators such as:
   - ability to transmit and receive complete originator/beneficiary data;
   - frequency of missing or incomplete information;
   - treatment of repeated non-compliant counterparties;
   - controls for transfers involving self-hosted addresses;
   - interoperability and data-quality problems.

   The EU Travel Rule guidelines have applied since 30 December 2024. Sources: [EBA Travel Rule Guidelines](https://www.eba.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2024-07/6de6e9b9-0ed9-49cd-985d-c0834b5b4356/Travel%20Rule%20Guidelines.pdf) and [FIN-FSA application notice](https://www.finanssivalvonta.fi/en/publications-and-press-releases/supervision-releases/2024/eba-guidelines-ebagl202411-on-information-requirements-in-relation-to-transfers-of-funds-and-certain-crypto-assets-transfers-under-regulation-eu-20231113-travel-rule-guidelines--applicable-from-30-december-2024/).

3. **Add a sanctions/restrictive-measures exposure dimension**

   The EBA restrictive-measures guidelines have applied since 30 December 2025 and require institutions to conduct exposure assessments and tailor controls accordingly. Relevant client-level indicators include:
   - direct and indirect ownership/control exposure;
   - business involving sanctioned or circumvention-sensitive jurisdictions;
   - opaque intermediaries, nominee structures and rapid ownership changes;
   - products or transaction routes capable of obscuring origin, destination or counterparties;
   - the client’s own sanctions-screening governance and effectiveness—particularly for CASPs.

   Keep this as a **separate sanctions exposure score or mandatory overlay**, rather than assuming that the AML score adequately captures sanctions risk. Source: [EBA Guidelines on restrictive-measures controls](https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/single-rulebook/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism/guidelines-internal-policies-procedures-and-controls-ensure-implementation-union-and-national).

4. **Capture technology and control-dependency risk**

   The EBA’s 2025 risk opinion highlights vulnerabilities involving crypto, FinTech, careless use of RegTech, AI-enabled fraud and sanctions compliance. Consider scoring:
   - material outsourcing of CDD, screening or transaction monitoring;
   - model governance, validation and human oversight;
   - data coverage and lineage;
   - alert backlogs, overrides and unresolved control deficiencies;
   - exposure to AI-enabled impersonation, synthetic identities or account-compromise typologies.

   Source: [EBA 2025 Opinion on ML/TF risks affecting the EU financial sector](https://www.eba.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2025-07/13ae2f94-dc04-4a50-9f24-af2808e78944/Opinion%20and%20Report%20on%20ML%20TF%20risks.pdf).

5. **Avoid category-based automatic high-risk outcomes**

   A CASP classification can be an important inherent-risk factor, but the assessment should distinguish between individual firms based on their services, jurisdictions, client base and controls. EBA guidance opposes blanket refusal or termination based solely on membership of a higher-risk client category. Source: [Guidelines on access to financial services](https://www.amf-france.org/en/regulation/policy/doc-2024-02#xts=607212&xtor=RSS-39&type=RSS).

### Design changes worth making

- Separate **inherent risk**, **control effectiveness** and **residual risk**.
- Introduce event-driven reassessment triggers: licensing change, new jurisdiction/service, enforcement action, ownership change, material Travel Rule failures or sharp transactional deviation.
- Prevent strong controls from fully neutralising certain red flags through score caps or mandatory escalation.
- Record the rationale for expert overrides and back-test scoring against alerts, SARs, exits and control incidents.
- Align client scoring with the business-wide assessment across customers, products, transactions, delivery channels and geography. AMLA’s current BWRA guidance remains draft, but it is useful for future-proofing the architecture: [AMLA consultation on draft BWRA Guidelines](https://www.amla.europa.eu/policy/public-consultations/consultation-draft-guidelines-business-wide-risk-assessment_en).

For your portfolio, the biggest likely gap is a CASP score that reflects only “industry = high risk” and does not separately measure **authorisation status, service model, wallet/counterparty exposure, Travel Rule performance, sanctions controls and actual control effectiveness**.

Yes. The biggest change this cycle is that crypto-client risk should move beyond “VASP registration + standard AML controls” to a more granular assessment of authorisation, business model, transfer controls and operational resilience.

### Priority additions

1. **MiCA status as a time-sensitive gating factor**  
   Record the client’s MiCA authorisation status, authorised services, home regulator, passporting footprint and any remaining reliance on national transitional arrangements. The transition ends on **1 July 2026**; unauthorised providers are expected to cease relevant EU activity and have executable wind-down plans ([AMF — End of the MiCA transitional period](https://www.amf-france.org/fr/actualites-publications/actualites/fin-de-la-periode-transitoire-mica-lesma-precise-ses-attentes-envers-les-professionnels-et-alerte)).  
   - Make “application pending” materially different from “authorised”.  
   - Introduce an immediate review trigger for refusal, withdrawal, scope limitation or missed deadline.  
   - After 1 July, treat continued EU activity without authorisation as critical legal/regulatory risk; determine whether banking should be limited to an orderly exit rather than applying an indiscriminate account closure.

2. **Crypto-specific inherent-risk factors**  
   The EBA expressly added crypto-specific factors for institutions assessing CASPs, including transaction, product, customer-behaviour and high-risk-jurisdiction exposure ([EBA — Guidelines amending ML/TF risk-factor guidelines](https://www.eba.europa.eu/legacy/regulation-and-policy/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-1)). Score separately:
   - custody, exchange, transfer, trading-platform and proprietary-trading activities;
   - retail versus institutional customer base;
   - self-hosted-wallet interaction;
   - cross-chain bridges, DeFi, staking, lending and leveraged products;
   - privacy-enhancing assets/services and weakly supervised counterparties;
   - nested or omnibus activity where the underlying customer is not transparent;
   - exposure to high-risk or sanctioned jurisdictions.

3. **Travel Rule performance—not merely policy existence**  
   Since 30 December 2024, controls should detect and manage transfers with missing or incomplete originator/beneficiary information, including self-hosted-wallet scenarios ([EBA — Travel Rule Guidelines](https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/single-rulebook/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism/guidelines-information-requirements-relation-transfers-funds-and-certain-crypto-assets-transfers)). Assess:
   - percentage of transfers with complete data;
   - rejection, suspension and escalation rates;
   - counterparty-CASP due diligence;
   - interoperability gaps;
   - controls over self-hosted addresses;
   - evidence that repeated deficient counterparties are restricted.

4. **Control effectiveness demonstrated through data**  
   MiCA authorisation should not be treated as an AML “clean bill of health”. AMLA expects effective AML/CFT systems from the start of authorisation ([AMLA — High standards against financial crime in the crypto sector](https://www.amla.europa.eu/news-media/news-articles/amla-expects-high-standards-against-financial-crime-crypto-sector_en)). Request operating metrics, not just documents:
   - blockchain-analytics coverage by chain and asset;
   - sanctions-screening latency and coverage;
   - alert volumes, ageing and disposition;
   - STR quality and timeliness;
   - model validation and tuning;
   - manual-override rates;
   - oversight of outsourced transaction monitoring.

5. **Stablecoin exposure**  
   Identify whether the client issues, lists, distributes or settles using ARTs/EMTs; verify issuer status, white papers and the client’s process for removing non-compliant tokens. ESMA has highlighted cessation of services involving non-MiCA-compliant stablecoins ([ESMA — Crypto-assets and financial stability](https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2025-04/ESMA50-43599798-27866_Natasha_Cazenave_s_opening_statement_at_ECON_hearing_on_crypto-assets_and_financial_stability__8_April_2025.pdf)). Also capture reserve-bank concentration where the client is an issuer, as this can create liquidity and contagion risk.

6. **Market-conduct and conflicts risk**  
   For exchanges and trading platforms, incorporate market-abuse surveillance, employee dealing, token-listing governance, issuer relationships, proprietary trading and social-media monitoring. MiCA now includes market-abuse prevention and detection expectations ([ESMA — Final MiCA policy documents](https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2024-12/ESMA71-545613100-2676_ESMA_releases_last_policy_documents_to_get_ready_for_MiCA_-_Press_Release.pdf)).

7. **Operational resilience and concentration**  
   Assess custody architecture, key management, incident history, outsourcing, cloud/blockchain-node concentration, recovery testing and wind-down capability. DORA requires ICT-risk management, incident reporting, resilience testing and third-party controls ([AMF — DORA](https://www.amf-france.org/sites/institutionnel/files/pdf/74926/en/The_Regulation_on_Digital_Operational_Resilience_in_the_Financial_Sector_%28DORA%29.pdf?1778483975)).

8. **Third-country and reverse-solicitation risk**  
   Capture where clients are actually marketed to and onboarded—not just legal domicile. The reverse-solicitation exemption is narrow and applies where the EU client is the exclusive initiator ([ESMA — Reverse solicitation under MiCA](https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2024-12/ESMA71-545613100-2676_ESMA_releases_last_policy_documents_to_get_ready_for_MiCA_-_Press_Release.pdf)). UK-facing clients should also be assessed for FCA registration and financial-promotion compliance; the FCA is actively enforcing against illegal crypto promotions ([FCA — Action against HTX](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/press-releases/fca-action-against-htx-illegal-financial-promotions)).

### Methodology design

I would structure the rating as:

- **Inherent risk:** activities, assets, customers, jurisdictions, delivery channels and transaction profile.
- **Control effectiveness:** authorisation, governance, Travel Rule, KYT/blockchain analytics, sanctions, outsourcing and resilience.
- **Residual risk:** with hard overrides for unauthorised activity, serious supervisory action, sanctions-control failure or materially misleading information.
- **Dynamic triggers:** licence decisions, new jurisdictions/products/chains, stablecoin delistings, cyber incidents, abnormal fiat-flow changes and enforcement action.

The immediate practical step is a targeted refresh of every transitional-status crypto client before **1 July 2026**, supported by regulator-register evidence and a credible post-deadline operating or wind-down plan.

