### What to get right

1. **Explain processing before or when it starts**
   Use a short, layered notice in onboarding, with the full notice one tap away. State plainly:
   - what data is collected, including data collected automatically;
   - each purpose and legal basis;
   - required versus optional data and what happens if it is not provided;
   - sharing with vendors/partners and relevant international transfers;
   - retention periods;
   - profiling or automated decisions;
   - user rights, withdrawal/objection routes, DPO contact and complaint route.

   Information must be concise, intelligible, easily accessible and in clear language—especially for children. See [EDPB Guidelines on transparency under Regulation 2016/679](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/transparency_en).

2. **Do not turn the privacy notice into blanket consent**
   Acceptance of terms and agreement to optional analytics, personalisation or marketing should be separate. Where consent is used, it must be freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous—not bundled, coerced or a condition for unrelated functionality. See [EDPB Guidelines 05/2020 on consent](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/guidelines-052020-consent-under-regulation-2016679_en).

3. **Make choices genuinely neutral**
   Avoid preselected optional processing, prominent “accept” buttons paired with hidden or multi-step refusal, guilt-inducing wording, repeated prompts, or blocking progress after refusal. Regulators have required removal of interfaces that mislead or pressure users regarding consent: [EDPB—TikTok ordered to eliminate unfair design practices](https://edpb.europa.eu/news/news/2023/following-edpb-decision-tiktok-ordered-eliminate-unfair-design-practices-concerning_en).

4. **Design a real child journey**
   Do not rely solely on an adult-length legal notice. Provide child-readable explanations, short sentences, concrete examples and age-appropriate visuals. Test comprehension with children in relevant age bands. Privacy-protective options should be the default; optional tracking, discoverability, precise location and similar features should start off.

5. **Resolve age and parental-authorisation logic before design**
   If consent is the legal basis for an online service offered directly to a child, the applicable age rules require country-specific analysis. I did not find a reliable corpus record listing every current Member State threshold, so legal/privacy should validate the country matrix rather than the product hard-coding one EU-wide rule.

   Any age-assurance method should be proportionate and collect only what is needed—preferably an “above/below threshold” result rather than identity or full birth date. The EDPB also calls for risk assessment, appropriate DPIAs, reliability and accessible redress: [EDPB Statement 1/2025 on age assurance](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/statement/statement-12025-on-age-assurance_en).

6. **Handle SDK and device tracking before activation**
   Analytics, advertising IDs, fingerprinting and similar access to information on a device may require prior consent unless strictly necessary; the rule extends beyond conventional cookies. See [EDPB Guidelines 2/2023 on Article 5(3) of the ePrivacy Directive](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/guidelines-22023-technical-scope-art-53-eprivacy-directive_en). Ensure SDKs do not transmit data while the relevant choice is still pending.

7. **Check whether the DSA applies**
   If the app qualifies as an online platform accessible to minors, it must provide a high level of minors’ privacy, safety and security. Commission guidance recommends measures including private-by-default minor accounts and safer recommender-system and interaction controls: [Commission guidelines on protection of minors](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors). Whether your app is an “online platform” depends on its functionality.

