### Onboarding requirements

1. **Provide meaningful information before or when data is collected**
   Use a short, layered notice: an immediate summary with links to detail. It should identify:
   - what data is collected;
   - why and under which legal basis;
   - required versus optional fields and consequences of not providing them;
   - recipients, international transfers and retention;
   - profiling or automated decisions;
   - user rights, complaint route and contact details.

   Language must be concise, accessible and plain—particularly for children. See the EDPB’s [Guidelines on transparency under Regulation 2016/679](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/transparency_en).

2. **Do not turn the privacy notice into blanket consent**
   Determine a lawful basis separately for every purpose. Where consent is used:
   - make it opt-in, specific and granular by purpose;
   - do not preselect choices or bundle optional processing with service acceptance;
   - avoid pressure, misleading button hierarchy or unnecessary friction;
   - make withdrawal as easy as giving consent;
   - do not block core service functionality unless the processing is genuinely necessary.

   See the EDPB’s [Guidelines 05/2020 on consent](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/guidelines-052020-consent-under-regulation-2016679_en).

3. **Design specifically for children**
   Provide a child-facing explanation rather than assuming the adult notice is sufficient. Use short sentences, concrete examples, illustrations where useful, and explain the consequences of choices. Test comprehension with children in the relevant age ranges.

   If consent is your lawful basis for a child’s use, obtain any legally required parental authorisation. The applicable consent threshold needs a country-by-country check; I did not find a reliable EU Member State threshold table in the corpus.

4. **Use proportionate, privacy-preserving age assurance**
   Do not automatically collect an exact birth date, ID document or biometrics if an age-band signal will do. Age assurance should be necessary, proportionate, reliable and provide a correction/redress route. Assess it in the DPIA before implementation. See the EDPB’s [Statement 1/2025 on age assurance](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/statement/statement-12025-on-age-assurance_en).

5. **Delay non-essential tracking**
   Analytics, advertising SDKs, fingerprinting and similar access to device information generally require consent unless strictly necessary. Do not initialise those SDKs before the choice is made. See the EDPB’s [Guidelines 2/2023 on Article 5(3) of the ePrivacy Directive](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/guidelines-22023-technical-scope-art-53-eprivacy-directive_en).

6. **Default to the least data-intensive configuration**
   Ask for device permissions only when the feature is used, explain why immediately beforehand, and make optional permissions skippable. Avoid collecting fields “for future use.” Manipulative interfaces conflict with privacy by default; see the AEPD’s [Dark patterns: Manipulation in Internet services](https://www.aepd.es/en/prensa-y-comunicacion/blog/dark-patterns-manipulation-in-internet-services).

7. **Check whether the DSA online-platform rules apply**
   If users can publish or share content, interact publicly, or otherwise use a platform function, assess DSA coverage. Platforms accessible to minors are expected to provide high privacy, safety and security, including private defaults and child-safe interface design; profiling-based advertising to known minors is prohibited. See the Commission’s [Guidelines on the protection of minors](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors).

### Practical release gates

Before launch, I would require:

- a screen-by-screen data and SDK inventory;
- lawful-basis and consent mapping for each purpose;
- child and adult variants of the notice;
- a Member State parental-consent assessment;
- confirmation that optional SDKs remain off until consent;
- updated DPIA/children’s-rights risk assessment;
- usability tests checking comprehension and absence of nudging;
- evidence logs showing the notice and choices presented to each app version.

Yes. Treat the discontinuation and supplier-change pause as **two regulated transition projects**, with the following gates.

### 1. Complete an EU supply-interruption assessment now

For each affected device/model and Member State, document:

- expected last-supply or interruption date and duration;
- available manufacturer and channel inventory;
- market share, installed base and clinical criticality;
- suitable alternatives or successor devices, including training/infrastructure constraints;
- whether non-availability could cause death, serious deterioration or inability to provide treatment.

For Article 10a purposes, an interruption lasting **more than 60 days** is the general reporting indicator, although a shorter interruption can qualify where serious harm is possible. A fully adequate stockpile or suitable successor may mean notification is not required, but that conclusion should be documented. Critical-care products merit a conservative assessment. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/b431b10f-8512-4f47-9191-e1b84b2f9a27_en?filename=mdr_qna-article10a_mdr-ivdr_en.pdf))

### 2. Reserve the six-month notification window

If serious harm or its risk is reasonably foreseeable, the manufacturer must normally notify:

- **ANSM**, because the manufacturer is established in France; and
- every distributor/importer, hospital and healthcare professional supplied **directly** by the manufacturer.

Notification should use the MDCG 2024-16 Manufacturer Information Form and normally occur at least **six months before** the interruption or discontinuation. Distributors then cascade the manufacturer’s communication downstream without undue delay. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1860/oj/eng))

**Timing implication:** as of **28 July 2026**, any transition beginning before **28 January 2027** is already inside the ordinary six-month window. Exceptional-circumstances treatment is intended for genuinely sudden or unexpected events, not planned commercial or supplier transitions. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/b431b10f-8512-4f47-9191-e1b84b2f9a27_en?filename=mdr_qna-article10a_mdr-ivdr_en.pdf))

ANSM can request further information and take proportionate continuity measures, so keep the impact analysis, stock data, alternatives and mitigation plan readily available. ([legifrance.gouv.fr](https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/id/LEGIARTI000051561931/2025-05-03?utm_source=openai))

### 3. Clear the component-supplier change before cutover

Run formal design/QMS change control covering, as applicable:

- new-supplier qualification, audit and quality agreement;
- component equivalence and specification changes;
- risk-management and technical-documentation updates;
- biocompatibility, electrical, mechanical, sterilisation, packaging, transport and shelf-life impact;
- process validation, incoming inspection and lot-release criteria;
- traceability between old- and new-supplier production;
- validation lots and contingency inventory.

Submit the change to the notified body **before implementation** if it is substantial or could affect conformity, safety, performance or the certified device range. Also check the notified-body contract, which may require notification of a broader set of supplier or component changes. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?qid=1677453341650&uri=CELEX%3A32017R0745&utm_source=openai))

If either product is still a **legacy device**, specifically assess whether the supplier/component change is a significant design change that could jeopardise its transitional status. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2017/745/2026-01-01/eng?utm_source=openai))

### 4. Put the continuity package in place

Before confirming transition dates, line up:

- last-time production and safety-stock quantities;
- allocation rules for hospitals and vulnerable populations;
- successor/alternative-device capacity and customer training;
- distributor acknowledgement and cascade evidence;
- service, repairs, spare parts and consumables for the installed base;
- warranty and tender/supply-contract notice requirements;
- restart acceptance criteria and contingency plans if new-supplier validation slips.

### 5. Plan the regulatory closeout

- Update applicable actor, device/UDI and certificate information in **EUDAMED**; the relevant first four modules have been mandatory since **28 May 2026**. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/medical-devices-eudamed/overview_en?utm_source=openai))
- Continue PMS, vigilance and complaint handling for devices already in the field throughout their lifetime. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2017/745/oj/eng?utm_source=openai))
- Retain technical documentation, declarations and certificates for at least **10 years after the last device is placed on the market**, or **15 years for implantables**. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN-CS/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32017R0745&utm_source=openai))
- Run separate country checks for European markets outside the EU/EEA, such as the UK and Switzerland.

**Recommended sequence:** regulatory-impact decision → notified-body engagement → demand/stock and clinical-impact assessment → Article 10a/ANSM notification where applicable → supplier validation and approval → inventory build → customer transition → production stop/cutover → EUDAMED and lifecycle closeout.

Yes. Put a **regulatory and continuity gate** ahead of each commercial transition date.

### 1. Assess shortage-notification duties first
For each device and country, document whether the interruption or discontinuation could create serious harm because alternatives, inventory, or clinical capacity are insufficient. For critical-care products, assume this requires careful evidence rather than treating it as a routine commercial exit.

If EU MDR Article 10a applies, notify:

- **ANSM**, because the manufacturer is established in France;
- directly supplied distributors/importers;
- directly supplied hospitals and healthcare professionals.

Notification is generally required **at least six months before supply stops**, using the MDCG manufacturer information form. Downstream economic operators must cascade the notice. A production pause normally does not require notification if stock can fully bridge demand; interruptions exceeding roughly 60 days are the general trigger for assessment, although shorter shortages can qualify when patient harm is possible. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1860/oj/eng?utm_source=openai))

**Timing implication:** as of **July 28, 2026**, any qualifying supply interruption before **January 28, 2027** is already inside the normal six-month window.

### 2. Clear the component-supplier change before committing dates
Run the change through formal design/QMS change control, including:

- new-supplier qualification, audit and quality agreement;
- specification and equivalence assessment;
- risk-management and clinical-impact updates;
- required process, performance, biocompatibility, sterilization, packaging, shelf-life or electrical testing;
- incoming inspection, traceability and first-batch release controls;
- technical-documentation, labeling and UDI impact;
- dual-source or rollback plan.

Check the notified-body agreement early. Manufacturers must submit relevant planned changes for prior assessment where required; the MDR QMS must include supplier control and device-modification procedures. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?qid=1496918076971&uri=CELEX%3A32017R0745&utm_source=openai))

For qualifying planned changes, current EU rules allow the notified body up to **30 days for initial review**, a further **90 days for additional assessment**, and potentially **20 days for a certificate supplement**. Those clocks can pause while questions or nonconformities are resolved, so build contingency into the schedule. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/PDF/?uri=OJ%3AL_202600977))

If either line is still a **legacy device**, separately confirm that the supplier change is not a prohibited significant change in design or intended purpose, and obtain notified-body confirmation where uncertain. ([health.ec.europa.eu](https://health.ec.europa.eu/latest-updates/update-mdcg-2020-3-rev1-guidance-significant-changes-regarding-transitional-provision-under-article-2023-05-12_en?prefLang=lv))

### 3. Build the continuity package
Before announcing either transition, agree:

- last-production and last-order dates;
- safety-stock assumptions by country and customer;
- allocation rules if demand exceeds stock;
- successor-device availability and clinical equivalence;
- hospital conversion, validation and training lead times;
- distributor inventory, consignment, returns and obsolescence treatment;
- tender, framework-agreement, service-contract and customer-notice requirements;
- contingency if the new supplier misses validation or approval.

Keep the **production-stop date** separate from the **last-supply date**—Article 10a is concerned with supply availability.

### 4. Plan the installed-base tail
Discontinuing sales does not end lifecycle responsibilities. Fund and assign ownership for:

- complaints, vigilance, recalls and field actions;
- post-market surveillance for devices still in use;
- spare parts, consumables, calibration, software/cybersecurity and technical support;
- document and sample retention;
- liability coverage and customer communications.

MDR technical documentation must generally be retained for at least **10 years after the last device is placed on the market**, or **15 years for implantables**, and post-market surveillance covers the device’s lifetime. ([eur-lex.europa.eu](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A02017R0745-20250110))

### 5. Close registrations and country-specific actions
At discontinuation, update the device’s market status in the applicable registration system. ANSM guidance requires cessation to be recorded at the time commercialization stops; if the original registration was in EUDAMED, the cessation is updated there. Also check national shortage, tender and reimbursement requirements in every non-EU market served. ([ansm.sante.fr](https://ansm.sante.fr/uploads/2023/08/08/20230808-notice-explicative-dm.pdf))

**Recommended sequencing:** Article 10a assessment → notified-body/change classification → validation and approval → inventory/successor confirmation → customer and authority notifications → production change or last build → installed-base support.

