Yes. Given that the app includes under-16s, I would treat the following as release gates. A definitive sign-off still depends on what this release changes—especially messaging, recommendations, companion behaviour, subscriptions, advertising, data collection, and image generation.

### Build before shipping

1. **Reliable age banding**
   - Do not rely only on a date-of-birth declaration.
   - Support privacy-preserving age assurance and distinguish at least child/adult experiences.
   - Use stronger checks wherever mature or otherwise harmful content is available.

   UK regulators expressly reject ineffective self-declaration and expect proportionate age assurance: [Joint Statement from ICO and Ofcom on Age Assurance](https://ico.org.uk/about-the-ico/media-centre/news-and-blogs/2026/03/joint-statement-from-ico-and-ofcom-on-age-assurance/). Italy/EU guidance likewise recommends age assurance for platforms accessible to minors: [Commission guidelines on the protection of minors](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors).

2. **A child-safe product mode**
   For known or likely minors:
   - private accounts by default;
   - no unsolicited adult contact;
   - block, mute and child-friendly reporting controls;
   - consent before group additions;
   - safer recommendations that reduce harmful content and prioritise explicit user choices;
   - no profiling-based advertising to minors;
   - avoid manipulative or dependency-driving prompts.

   These measures track the EU Commission’s DSA guidance: [Commission guidelines on the protection of minors](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors).

3. **Companion safety guardrails**
   Test and block outputs involving:
   - sexual interaction or sexualisation of minors;
   - grooming;
   - encouragement of suicide or self-harm;
   - reinforcement of delusions or dangerous conduct;
   - illegal gambling;
   - CSAM and non-consensual sexual imagery.

   California is actively enforcing prohibitions on AI-generated sexual material involving minors: [AG Bonta cease-and-desist action against xAI](https://www.oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/attorney-general-bonta-sends-cease-and-desist-letter-xai-demands-it-halt-illegal).

4. **Clear AI disclosure**
   Every companion experience should unmistakably tell users that they are interacting with AI—not a human. Complete this now if the release will remain live after **2 August 2026**, when EU AI Act chatbot transparency requirements apply: [EU AI Act regulatory framework](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai).

5. **Privacy controls and child-readable notices**
   Build:
   - concise, age-appropriate privacy explanations;
   - data minimisation;
   - deletion, access and objection controls;
   - an explicit choice over whether conversations are used for model training;
   - parental-consent handling where your processing relies on consent and the user is below the applicable digital-consent age;
   - a DPIA covering children, companion conversations, safety monitoring and age assurance.

   The UK ICO has already fined a platform for missing age checks, under-13 parental consent and a DPIA: [Imgur owner fined over children’s privacy failures](https://ico.org.uk/about-the-ico/media-centre/news-and-blogs/2026/02/imgur-owner-medialab-fined-over-children-s-privacy-failures/). Italy’s regulator has also required AI services to provide transparency, training opt-outs and effective age controls: [Italian Garante—privacy and age-verification changes](https://www.garanteprivacy.it/garante/doc.jsp?ID=9882865).

6. **Moderation, reporting and appeals**
   Include easy reporting of illegal content, bullying and unsafe companion replies; block/mute tools; timely case handling; and an appeal route for moderation decisions. These are core DSA platform controls: [DSA—Making the online world safer](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/safer-online).

### California items requiring immediate legal confirmation

- California’s **AB 56** requires periodic social-media warning labels for children and teens: [California AG summary](https://www.oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/attorney-general-bonta-throws-support-behind-colorado-amid-challenge-its-social). Confirm whether the app falls within the statutory definition and implement the prescribed warning cadence if it does.
- California DOJ has proposed age-assurance and parental-consent rules under **SB 976**: [Proposed SB 976 regulations](https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/california-department-justice-releases-proposed-protecting-our-kids-social-media). These are proposed rules, so do not treat that release alone as the final technical specification.
- I could not locate an authoritative companion-chatbot statute in the corpus matching the expected suicide/self-harm, break-notice and reporting provisions. That specific California issue should be checked by counsel before release rather than assuming it is inapplicable.

The UK’s proposed under-16 social-media restriction is also not yet a current blanket release requirement; it remains a government proposal. Existing UK age-assurance, Children’s Code and Online Safety Act duties still apply.

