Yes. Based on the product and markets, I would treat the following as **release blockers**.

## 1. Cross-market age handling

Build a jurisdiction-aware age flow that can:

- distinguish adults from minors with assurance proportionate to the risk—not merely an unchecked date-of-birth field for high-risk companion functionality;
- apply child protections by default when age is uncertain;
- obtain and record parental consent for Italian users under 14;
- route minors to age-appropriate models, content controls and privacy settings;
- support challenges where age estimation is wrong; and
- minimise and promptly delete age-verification data.

Italy’s AI law requires parental consent before under-14s access AI technology. Users aged 14–17 can provide their own consent for associated personal-data processing only where information is accessible and understandable. ([senato.it](https://www.senato.it/show-doc?id=1418921&leg=19&part=ddlpres_ddlpres1-articolato_articolato1-capo_capoii-articolo_articolo13&parthttp=ddlpres_ddlpres1-articolato_articolato1-capo_capoi-articolo_articolo4&tipodoc=DDLPRES&utm_source=openai))

## 2. California companion-chatbot controls

California’s SB 243 has applied since **January 1, 2026**. Before shipping, implement:

- a clear disclosure that companions are AI and not human;
- for every known minor, an AI reminder and break prompt **at least every three hours** of continuous interaction;
- an in-product warning that companion chatbots may not be suitable for some minors;
- reasonable controls preventing sexually explicit visual output to minors and statements encouraging minors to engage in sexually explicit conduct;
- a tested self-harm/suicide detection and response protocol, including crisis-service referrals;
- a public webpage describing that protocol; and
- event logging that can support the anonymised annual reporting beginning **July 1, 2027**.

Noncompliance carries a private right of action, including at least $1,000 per violation where injury results. ([leginfo.legislature.ca.gov](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?chapter=22.6&division=8.&lawCode=BPC&part=&title=&utm_source=openai))

## 3. EU AI Act transparency

For Italy, Article 50 applies from **August 2, 2026**:

- tell users clearly, accessibly and from the beginning of the first interaction that they are interacting with AI;
- implement machine-readable marking/detection for generated text, images, audio and video;
- visibly label deepfakes or public-interest AI content where applicable.

There is **no general grace period for the chatbot disclosure**. The limited transition to **December 2, 2026** only concerns machine-readable marking for systems already placed on the market before August 2. ([digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/transparency-obligations-under-article-50-ai-act?utm_source=openai))

Also review engagement optimisation, emotional dependency mechanics and personalised persuasion. The AI Act already prohibits AI that exploits age-related vulnerability or uses manipulative techniques in ways that materially distort behaviour and cause, or are reasonably likely to cause, significant harm. ([ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-5?utm_source=openai))

## 4. Child-safe privacy defaults

For child accounts, ship with:

- profiles and activity private by default;
- location sharing off;
- nonessential profiling, targeted advertising and cross-service sharing off;
- no interface nudges encouraging weaker privacy;
- child-readable privacy notices and controls;
- deletion/export/account controls appropriate for children and parents; and
- a completed DPIA covering companion memory, inferred emotions/interests, safety classifiers, model training and age assurance.

The UK Children’s Code applies to services likely to be accessed by children, even when children are not the target audience. ([ico.org.uk](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/childrens-information/childrens-code-guidance-and-resources/introduction-to-the-childrens-code/?utm_source=openai)) Italy’s regulator has just penalised an AI companion provider for inadequate age controls, public-by-default minor profiles, transparency failures and a late DPIA. ([gpdp.it](https://gpdp.it/web/guest/home/docweb/-/docweb-display/docweb/10269571))

## 5. UK Online Safety Act release gate

Because this is a social chat app, assume it is a regulated user-to-user service unless counsel confirms otherwise. Before any significant release:

- update and approve the children’s risk assessment;
- test exposure to pornography, suicide, self-harm, eating-disorder, violent, bullying and other harmful content;
- prevent children from encountering primary-priority harmful content;
- provide prominent reporting, blocking and complaints mechanisms;
- document moderation, recommender and age-assurance controls; and
- update the terms to explain child-safety measures.

Ofcom expressly requires a fresh assessment **before a significant service change**. ([ofcom.org.uk](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/online-safety/protecting-children/protection-of-children-duties-under-the-online-safety-act))

## 6. Italy/EU social-platform protections

If the app qualifies as an online platform and you are not within the micro/small-business exemption, the DSA requires a high level of privacy, safety and security for minors. The Commission’s compliance benchmark includes:

- private-by-default accounts;
- effective age assurance;
- block/mute and unwanted-contact controls;
- explicit consent before adding minors to groups;
- safer recommender systems and anti-rabbit-hole controls;
- restrictions on addictive design; and
- no targeted advertising to minors based on profiling. ([digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors?utm_source=openai))

## Recommended ship decision

**Do not ship** unless the release checklist has signed-off evidence for:

1. age assurance and Italian parental consent;
2. California disclosures, three-hour reminders and crisis protocol;
3. August 2 EU AI disclosure;
4. minor privacy/safety defaults;
5. updated UK child-risk assessment; and
6. red-team results covering sexual content, grooming, self-harm, eating disorders, dependency and attempts to bypass age controls.

Also confirm whether under-13s are admitted. If so, a separate COPPA parental-notice and verifiable-consent flow is required before collecting their personal information. ([ftc.gov](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions?utm_source=openai))
